ine right now  and so people think my colleagues have  looked at this they think this is the  biggest shock to global wheat markets  since the first world war potentially so  this is this we're going to notice this  when you go to the restaurant when you  go to the grocery store people will feel  more of this the longer it goes on who  doesn't seem to care about the sanctions  though zanny do you think he does well  he's i mean it doesn't seem to have  changed his strategy whatever that is it  hasn't but and it's worth emphasizing  though just how dramatic the hit to  russia has been i mean it's we've  noticed it here but in russia  the economy is being really decimated  it's being frozen almost frozen in  aspect and the transformation that putin  has done in that country in the last  three weeks he's basically turned it  into a completely fascist dictatorship  you've you've had reports on the kind of  news that russians get it's total  propaganda the whole time it's the fight  for the fatherland against the west  they're not getting this they're not  getting the story  he's actually still pretty popular he's  framing it as plucky russia standing up  up against the kind of fascists in  ukraine and the question is can he do  that as his economy keeps collapsing and  and i think the lesson of history  unfortunately is that dictators can hang  on for a long time  he can't win he can't afford to win but  he can't afford to lose either i think  that's absolutely right i think right  now putin can't win in the sense that he  can't do what he thought he was going to  do really easily roll into ukraine be  greeted as a liberator you know kind of  hold the country the extraordinary  resistance the extraordinary courage of  the ukrainians and frankly the relative  incompetence of the russian military has  meant that he can't win but he also  can't afford to lose because he has  framed this as a kind of existential  struggle and so he needs to people talk  about off-ramps the 